Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD), yet single-pass prompting fails silently on domains where the VLM's world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition. We study how to add structure on top of a VLM for \emph{cross-domain} VAD and present two complementary contributions.

\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.

\textbf{Mechanism.} Controlled rank-preserving ablations localise the gain to \emph{score-rank granularity}, not to middle-zone mass. Direct emits only 11 unique score values on our test set whereas the v6 ReAct agent emits 49; the agent's finer rank grid breaks ties inside Direct's bimodal clusters when the two scores are averaged. A rank-preserving transformation that keeps the agent's 49 unique scores but pushes them all outside $[0.2, 0.8]$ preserves the ensemble gain ($+4.68$ pp) despite $0\%$ middle-zone mass; a rank-collapsing binarisation (two unique values) drops the gain to $+2.31$ pp; a random middle-mass noise partner (uniform in $[0.2, 0.8]$) gives $-3.4$ pp. Middle-zone mass is therefore correlated with ensemble gain only because it correlates with rank granularity; when we decouple them, granularity is what matters. The deeper ingredients are: (i) the agent's score has non-trivial standalone signal (random score with no signal hurts the ensemble); (ii) the agent has a distinct rank grid with many unique values; (iii) the agent's errors are complementary to the VLM's.

\textbf{Secondary contribution.} We introduce a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that implements an explicit ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, each is targeted by a refutation tool, surviving candidates yield anomaly evidence. v8 is interpretable---a reviewer can audit exactly which features survived and why---and gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (CI $[-0.001,+0.027]$) when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain relative to v6 is explained by the same rank-granularity mechanism: v8's explicit refutation protocol collapses scores to a small number of unique values ($0.05, 0.85$) which preserves correct rank on refuted vs surviving features but destroys rank granularity within each group.

\textbf{Takeaway.} Training-free VAD benefits from \emph{score-diverse} VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not by stronger standalone agents. We release the v6/v8 agent implementations, per-tool single-tool audit framework (13 tools, dev n=480), and full paired-bootstrap analysis artifacts.

