\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter protocol and sensitivity.}
All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.

\subsection{Negative results: why simpler agents fail}
\label{ssec:ablation}

We screened six alternative agent designs on the GPT-5.4 calibration split (20 items per domain, 240 items total) before arriving at the router. All uniformly regress the descriptor-enhanced direct baseline: self-refine $-5.9$ pp, naive debate $-7.2$ pp, gated debate $-4.1$ pp, evidence-injected proposer $-10.2$ pp, third-call arbiter $-9.1$ pp, normal-first $-2.4$ pp. The failure mode is consistent: an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call rationalises correct high-confidence predictions away. AnomalyClaw avoids this by (i) using an \emph{external} non-parametric expert as the escalation signal and (ii) using a router rather than a fixed sequence. Full variant table and per-variant failure analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}. These numbers should be read as \emph{design-screening evidence on one backbone's calibration split} rather than cross-backbone generalisation claims.

\subsection{Complementarity and budget}
\label{ssec:complementarity}
The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{ssec:limitations}
The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.

