paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:52:{\footnotesize $^{*}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){\geq}0.95$; $^{***}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$; $^\dagger$ GPT-5.4 v8 numbers are dev-only ($n{=}480$) because the sub2api endpoint repeatedly rate-limited v8's test-scale concurrency. See \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity} for the score-distribution analysis that explains the v6-vs-v8 ensemble gap.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:76:\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:77:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:79:\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:80:\label{ssec:v8_finding}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:81:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:83:\subsection{Why ensembles win: rank-granularity, not middle-mass}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:89:    \item \textbf{v6-BIN}: collapse v6 scores to two values $\{0.05, 0.95\}$ at the per-split median. Preserves sign but destroys rank granularity (2 unique values vs 49 in original).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:90:    \item \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: monotone-remap v6 scores to linearly spaced points in $[0.05, 0.19]\cup[0.81, 0.95]$, preserving every unique rank but moving all 49 values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$. Preserves rank granularity AND standalone AUROC; destroys middle-zone mass.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:91:    \item \textbf{v6-AFFINE($a$)}: $s\mapsto\mathrm{clip}(0.5 + (s-0.5)\cdot a, 0, 1)$. Preserves rank for $a\!\le\!1$ (no clipping) and tests rank collapse near the boundaries for $a\!>\!1$.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:92:    \item \textbf{v8-SOFT}: monotone compression of v8 scores into $[0.25, 0.75]$. Adds middle-zone mass while preserving rank.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:93:    \item \textbf{RANDOM}: replace agent scores with uniform samples in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Pure middle-zone noise with no signal.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:98:\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:103:Agent variant $X$ & $X$ alone & ensemble $\Delta$ & \# unique & mid-mass (\%) & $\rho_S$ & err-cos \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:111:v8 original           & $0.6710$ & $+1.22$ & $\sim\!15$ & $0.4\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.594$ \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:112:v8-SOFT               & $0.6710$ & $+1.35$ & $\sim\!15$ & $100\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.634$ \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:115:Direct only (reference) & $0.7684$ & --- & $11$ & $0.0\%$ & $1.0$ & $0.0$ \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:120:Two clean falsifications and one corroboration follow. (i) Middle-mass is \emph{not causal}: v6-EXT-RANK preserves every v6 rank but pushes all values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$; the ensemble gain is $+4.68$ pp, larger than the $+4.53$ pp of the original. (ii) Rank granularity \emph{is} causal: v6-BIN collapses 49 ranks to 2 and the gain halves. (iii) v6-AFFINE is the cleanest positive control---compressing $a<1$ preserves rank and stays in the high-gain regime ($+4.64$ to $+4.71$ pp) regardless of middle-mass; expanding $a>1$ clips out-of-range values and collapses ranks, dropping gain. On this evidence, the mechanism we report is: \emph{Direct has a coarse bimodal rank grid (11 unique values); averaging with an agent that has a fine rank grid (49 unique values) breaks ties inside each bimodal cluster with real signal}. Middle-mass and rank granularity happen to correlate on unprocessed VLM outputs because free-form JSON produces many distinct intermediate numerals; decoupling them in the lab separates the correlational from the causal.
