paper/sections/3_method.tex:23:Each tool has a small, auditable contract; all tools are zero-VLM-call. The \texttt{domain\_descriptor} returns the task-anchored anomaly specification for the current domain (resolving generic-prior/task conflicts, e.g.\ D08 ``planned urbanisation IS the anomaly''). The \texttt{reference\_retriever} ranks the reference pool by DINOv2-CLS cosine similarity and returns the top-$k$. The \texttt{hotspot\_cropper} reads top-$k$ patch coordinates from an expert and crops the anomaly hotspot (used by the \emph{interpret} strategy). The \texttt{component\_counter} runs connected-component analysis on the expert hotspot map (logical anomalies). The \texttt{knowledge\_lookup} retrieves a domain keyword list for the VLM prompt (e.g.\ \emph{lesion, haemorrhage} for medical). Full tool signatures, I/O contracts, and implementations are in Appendix~\ref{app:tools}.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:79:\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
paper/sections/3_method.tex:80:\label{ssec:refutation}
paper/sections/3_method.tex:82:Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---the VLM emits extreme scores with almost no middle-zone mass; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:85:The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
paper/sections/3_method.tex:87:    \item \textbf{Suspect list (turn 1, no tool).} The VLM outputs an \texttt{initial\_score}, a list of up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}~$=\{(\text{name}_i, \text{location\_hint}_i, \text{looks\_defect\_like}_i)\}$ and a \texttt{refutation\_target} index~$t^*$ into that list. The prompt fixes the default: if \texttt{candidate\_features} is empty and \texttt{initial\_score}~$<0.3$, the agent finalises at~$0.05$ without calling a tool.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:88:    \item \textbf{Targeted refutation (turn 2+).} The agent calls a refutation tool whose output is about \emph{whether the feature at $t^*$ appears in any reference}. \texttt{side\_by\_side} crops query and four refs at the same bbox; \texttt{reference\_profiler} returns a structured normal-baseline profile (object, colors, allowed variations); \texttt{reference\_retriever} fetches top-$k$ similar normals. Each output is paired with an explicit \texttt{interpretation} field containing a disconfirm clause, e.g.\ ``the bright region in the composite also appears in ref 2 at the same position.''
paper/sections/3_method.tex:89:    \item \textbf{Verdict and update.} The VLM emits \texttt{refutation\_verdict} $\in \{\text{found\_in\_ref}, \text{not\_found}, \text{inconclusive}\}$, removes refuted features from \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}, and outputs \texttt{updated\_score}. If the remaining list is empty the score is forced into $[0.05, 0.20]$; if at least one feature survives the score is in $[0.40, 0.95]$ proportional to its defect-likeness. Remaining features are checked in subsequent turns until budget is exhausted (max 5 turns).
paper/sections/3_method.tex:93:In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:95:\paragraph{Ensemble with Direct.}
paper/sections/3_method.tex:96:Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
paper/sections/3_method.tex:98:  s_{\mathrm{final}} \;=\; w \cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + (1-w) \cdot s_{\mathrm{agent}}
paper/sections/3_method.tex:100:with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:102:\paragraph{Score-distribution diversity.}
paper/sections/3_method.tex:103:Section~\ref{sec:experiments} shows the ensemble gain tracks a distributional quantity: the fraction of scores that land in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Direct is bimodal ($100$\% extreme), v6.5's looser JSON protocol produces $24$\% middle-zone outputs (a strong ensemble partner), v8's explicit refutation schema still lands $<1$\% in the middle. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} broadens the output distribution is the main open direction left by this work.
refine-logs/RESEARCH_IDEA.md:22:Score fusion (`0.8·s_v0 + 0.2·σ(s_exp)`) already beats direct on all three backbones (Qwen3.5 +5.5 pp, SeedVL +1.7 pp, GPT-5.4 +1.5 pp), so the simplest agent (apply per-domain fusion when expert is reliable, direct otherwise) should comfortably clear +5 pp on Qwen3.5 and meet or approach it on SeedVL/GPT-5.4.
refine-logs/RESEARCH_IDEA.md:50:| `fusion` | 1 | Direct + post-hoc blend with expert score (`s = (1−w)·s_v0 + w·σ(s_exp)`). |
refine-logs/RESEARCH_IDEA.md:51:| `zoom_fusion` | 1 | Direct call, but the VLM input is augmented with the expert hotspot crop as an additional image. Then post-hoc blend. |
refine-logs/RESEARCH_IDEA.md:52:| `retrieve_fusion` | 1 | Direct call with top-k retrieved refs (instead of random first-k). Then post-hoc blend. |
refine-logs/RESEARCH_IDEA.md:53:| `knowledge_fusion` | 1 | Direct call with knowledge_lookup keywords injected into the descriptor prompt. Then post-hoc blend. |
refine-logs/RESEARCH_IDEA.md:54:| `interpret` | ≤ 2 | Direct call; if v0 says normal AND expert hotspot is strong + concentrated, second call with crop. |
