refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3776:- [benchmark/scripts/compose_ensemble.py](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/compose_ensemble.py:38): ensemble fallback only handles missing scores, not errored records. Agent JSON failures still carry `anomaly_score=0.5`, so `compose_ensemble.py` averages Direct with synthetic 0.5 instead of falling back to Direct. In current Qwen v6.5+D this changes 6 items and slightly changes macro AUROC.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3786:- [benchmark/scripts/agent_tools_v6.py](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_tools_v6.py:92), [benchmark/scripts/agent_tools_v6.py](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_tools_v6.py:102): expert percentile rank is computed against the whole active split. On `split="test"`, that is a transductive use of the full test score distribution. It is label-free, but not a per-item online agent.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3787:  Exact fix: compute percentile ranks against calibration/train expert-score distributions, while looking up only the current item’s raw expert score from the active split.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3795:- [benchmark/scripts/agent_prompt_v6_6.py](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_prompt_v6_6.py:3), [benchmark/scripts/agent_v6_6.py](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_v6_6.py:137), [benchmark/scripts/infer.py](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/infer.py:459): score calibration documentation is internally inconsistent. v6.6 does not use `score_from_v0`; it averages free-form `initial_score` and free-form final `score`. Direct uses `score_from_v0`. v6.7’s Direct turn uses `score_from_v0`, while its ReAct final uses free score.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3814:As a paper about a pure autonomous visual anomaly-detection agent, this is not publishable yet. The cleanest result is not the agent: fixed Direct+SubspaceAD fusion matches or beats most claimed gains, and the strongest reported Qwen improvement is an offline ensemble selected after many test-set iterations. Reviewers will reasonably say the contribution is an ensemble/complementarity trick, not an agentic detection algorithm.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3816:A publishable angle is possible if the paper is reframed honestly: “tool-augmented VLM agents are brittle; simple fusion is a strong baseline; agents help only under identifiable reliability conditions.” For a positive agent paper, you need a frozen dev-selected method, a clean held-out test, no contaminated retrieval bank, and evidence that tool use causally improves rank ordering beyond Direct and fixed fusion.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3822:2. Run one-tool causal ablations: Direct-style visual judgment, then exactly one allowed tool family, then final free score. Compare `expert_score`, `reference_profiler`, `image_diff`, and `retriever` under the same K and prompt budget.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3826:4. Add a self-critique final step instead of post-hoc ensemble: the agent outputs final score plus a tool-reliability assessment, and must downweight any tool it labels unreliable. No external Direct averaging.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3828:5. Create a dev-only routing policy from non-label features available at inference: domain descriptor, first-turn uncertainty, expert raw/rank, and tool observation metadata. Freeze it, then evaluate once on held-out test as a pure agent controller.
