review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:391:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:393:\textbf{Mechanism.} We show through controlled ablations that the gain has three ingredients that are all necessary: (i) the agent's score has non-trivial standalone signal (random middle-mass noise gives $-3.4$ pp ensemble gain); (ii) the agent's score has substantial middle-zone mass (binarising the agent's output to $\{0.05, 0.95\}$ drops the ensemble by $2.2$ pp despite preserving rank); (iii) the agent's errors are complementary to the VLM's. Direct emits $0\%$ of scores in $[0.2, 0.8]$ whereas the v6 ReAct agent emits $24\%$, so averaging breaks ties within Direct's extreme clusters with real information. This mechanistic account is supported by rank correlation and error-cosine measurements.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:395:\textbf{Secondary contribution.} We introduce a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that implements an explicit ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, each is targeted by a refutation tool, surviving candidates yield anomaly evidence. v8 is interpretable---a reviewer can audit exactly which features survived and why---and gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (CI $[-0.001,+0.027]$) when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain relative to v6 is explained by the same middle-mass mechanism: v8's explicit refutation protocol pushes scores to $\{0.05, 0.85\}$, only $0.4\%$ of outputs land in the middle zone. A softened-score variant (v8-SOFT) closes part of the gap.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:397:\textbf{Takeaway.} Training-free VAD benefits from \emph{score-diverse} VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not by stronger standalone agents. We release the v6/v8 agent implementations, per-tool single-tool audit framework (13 tools, dev n=480), and full paired-bootstrap analysis artifacts.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:415:(i) \textbf{DINOv2-PatchNN}: patch-$k$-NN expert alone. (ii) \textbf{SubspaceAD}: PCA subspace expert alone~\citep{subspacead2026}. (iii) \textbf{Direct VLM} (v0): one VLM call with task-anchored descriptor. (iv) \textbf{Debate}: proposer--advocate, 2 VLM calls. (v) \textbf{Interpret}: AnomalyClaw v1 three-route router, $\sim 1.3$ VLM calls. (vi) \textbf{Fusion}: $s = 0.8\,s_0 + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$. (vii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Descriptor Router}: per-domain rule $S(d)$ derived from the family taxonomy (\S\ref{ssec:router}; simulated by score look-up on the existing per-strategy outputs). (viii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Calibration Router}: per-domain argmax strategy chosen from 20 calibration items per domain (simulated by score look-up). (ix) \textbf{AnomalyClaw v3 (real autonomous agent)}: end-to-end agent that runs the autonomous-planner VLM call per domain and executes the chosen tool/expert/strategy plan per item with the online expert override. (x) \textbf{Oracle}: per-domain argmax computed on test labels (upper bound; cheats).
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:425:\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
