review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:446:~~~~$\Delta$ vs Direct VLM (v6 ensemble) & $+1.74$ & $+0.93$ & $+4.53^{***}$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:447:~~~~$\Delta$ vs Direct VLM (v8 ensemble) & $+0.42^\dagger$ & --- & $+1.28^{*}$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:453:{\footnotesize $^{*}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){\geq}0.95$; $^{***}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$; $^\dagger$ GPT-5.4 v8 numbers are dev-only ($n{=}480$) because the sub2api endpoint repeatedly rate-limited v8's test-scale concurrency. See \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity} for the score-distribution analysis that explains the v6-vs-v8 ensemble gap.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:477:\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:478:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:480:\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:481:\label{ssec:v8_finding}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:482:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:489:    \item \textbf{v6-BIN}: rank-preserving binarisation of v6 scores to $\{0.05, 0.95\}$ via the per-item median. Removes middle-zone mass while preserving rank.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:490:    \item \textbf{v8-SOFT}: monotone compression $s \to 0.5 + (s-0.5)/2$, remaps v8 scores into $[0.25, 0.75]$. Adds middle-zone mass while preserving rank.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:491:    \item \textbf{RANDOM}: replace agent scores with uniform samples in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Pure middle-zone noise with no signal.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:496:\caption{Controlled score-diversity ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC across 12 domains). Middle-mass contributes substantially ($+2.2$ pp loss when binarised, row~2), but is not sufficient on its own (random middle-mass noise fails, row~5, and softening v8 recovers only $0.13$ pp, row~4).}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:501:Agent variant $X$ & $X$ alone & $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}{+}0.5\!\cdot\!X$ & mid-mass (\%) & Spearman $\rho$ & err-cos \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:503:v6.5 original     & $0.7713$ & $\mathbf{0.8136}$ ($\Delta\,+4.53$) & $24.0\%$ & $0.535$ & $0.621$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:505:v8 original       & $0.6710$ & $0.7806$ ($\Delta\,+1.22$)          & $0.4\%$  & $0.477$ & $0.594$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:506:v8 SOFT           & $0.6710$ & $0.7819$ ($\Delta\,+1.35$)          & $100.0\%$& $0.477$ & $0.634$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:509:Direct only (reference) & $0.7684$ & --- & $0.0\%$ & $1.0$ & $0.0$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:514:Three observations follow. (i) Binarising v6 (removing middle-mass while preserving rank) halves the ensemble gain from $+4.53$ to $+2.31$ pp. (ii) Softening v8 (gaining $100\%$ middle-mass while preserving rank) adds only $+0.13$ pp. (iii) Random middle-mass noise gives $-3.39$ pp. The gap between v6-BIN ($+2.31$ pp) and v6 original ($+4.53$ pp) is the \emph{middle-mass contribution}; the gap between v6-BIN and Direct-only ($+2.31$ pp) is the \emph{signal-with-complementary-errors contribution}. Middle-mass is necessary but not sufficient: it must be paired with signal that disagrees with Direct on different items than Direct's own errors. The monotone relationship between mid-mass (\%) and $\Delta$ in Table~\ref{tab:score_diversity} is causal only when signal is held constant, which the rank-preserving transformations test directly.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:593:Each tool has a small, auditable contract; all tools are zero-VLM-call. The \texttt{domain\_descriptor} returns the task-anchored anomaly specification for the current domain (resolving generic-prior/task conflicts, e.g.\ D08 ``planned urbanisation IS the anomaly''). The \texttt{reference\_retriever} ranks the reference pool by DINOv2-CLS cosine similarity and returns the top-$k$. The \texttt{hotspot\_cropper} reads top-$k$ patch coordinates from an expert and crops the anomaly hotspot (used by the \emph{interpret} strategy). The \texttt{component\_counter} runs connected-component analysis on the expert hotspot map (logical anomalies). The \texttt{knowledge\_lookup} retrieves a domain keyword list for the VLM prompt (e.g.\ \emph{lesion, haemorrhage} for medical). Full tool signatures, I/O contracts, and implementations are in Appendix~\ref{app:tools}.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:649:\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:650:\label{ssec:refutation}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:652:Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---the VLM emits extreme scores with almost no middle-zone mass; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:655:The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:657:    \item \textbf{Suspect list (turn 1, no tool).} The VLM outputs an \texttt{initial\_score}, a list of up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}~$=\{(\text{name}_i, \text{location\_hint}_i, \text{looks\_defect\_like}_i)\}$ and a \texttt{refutation\_target} index~$t^*$ into that list. The prompt fixes the default: if \texttt{candidate\_features} is empty and \texttt{initial\_score}~$<0.3$, the agent finalises at~$0.05$ without calling a tool.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:658:    \item \textbf{Targeted refutation (turn 2+).} The agent calls a refutation tool whose output is about \emph{whether the feature at $t^*$ appears in any reference}. \texttt{side\_by\_side} crops query and four refs at the same bbox; \texttt{reference\_profiler} returns a structured normal-baseline profile (object, colors, allowed variations); \texttt{reference\_retriever} fetches top-$k$ similar normals. Each output is paired with an explicit \texttt{interpretation} field containing a disconfirm clause, e.g.\ ``the bright region in the composite also appears in ref 2 at the same position.''
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:659:    \item \textbf{Verdict and update.} The VLM emits \texttt{refutation\_verdict} $\in \{\text{found\_in\_ref}, \text{not\_found}, \text{inconclusive}\}$, removes refuted features from \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}, and outputs \texttt{updated\_score}. If the remaining list is empty the score is forced into $[0.05, 0.20]$; if at least one feature survives the score is in $[0.40, 0.95]$ proportional to its defect-likeness. Remaining features are checked in subsequent turns until budget is exhausted (max 5 turns).
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:663:In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:665:\paragraph{Ensemble with Direct.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:666:Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:668:  s_{\mathrm{final}} \;=\; w \cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + (1-w) \cdot s_{\mathrm{agent}}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:670:with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:672:\paragraph{Score-distribution diversity.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:673:Section~\ref{sec:experiments} shows the ensemble gain tracks a distributional quantity: the fraction of scores that land in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Direct is bimodal ($100$\% extreme), v6.5's looser JSON protocol produces $24$\% middle-zone outputs (a strong ensemble partner), v8's explicit refutation schema still lands $<1$\% in the middle. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} broadens the output distribution is the main open direction left by this work.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:687:"""Controlled ablation: does middle-zone mass CAUSE ensemble gain, or
