review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2382:paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection agent organised around three orthogonal axes---\emph{Tools} (descriptor, retriever, cropper, counter, knowledge lookup), \emph{Experts} (SubspaceAD, DINOv2 patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2 global), and \emph{Strategies} (direct, fusion, debate, interpret)---and an autonomous router that selects a (tools, expert, strategy) plan per domain via a single text-only VLM call. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} with two VLM backbones (SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B), the real end-to-end agent matches the descriptor-only direct VLM baseline on both backbones (Δ ≤ 0.5 pp, n.s.) and ties score fusion on SeedVL (Δ = $-1.1$ pp, n.s.) while trailing fusion by 5.4 pp on Qwen3.5; per-domain it posts double-digit wins on semantic / logical domains where the planner correctly avoids fusion (D6 LEVIR change $+13.4$ pp on SeedVL). A simulated calibration-only router (which executes the per-domain planned strategy without the noisy online expert override) reaches $+0.8$/$+1.6$/$+0.2$ pp over the strongest single strategy on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5 (SeedVL significant), and a zero-data descriptor router recovers $+1.7$/$+2.2$/$+4.9$ pp over direct for free. Along the way, we documented three orthogonal findings: task-anchored descriptors dominate any agentic intervention ($+6.4$/$+4.1$/$+3.2$ pp, all significant); simple score fusion is a very strong baseline that prior agentic work underreported; and six failed-variant ablations show that \emph{unconditional} second-call escalation hurts when the baseline is already strong.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2387:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2393:paper/sections/3_method.tex:82:Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---the VLM emits extreme scores with almost no middle-zone mass; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2394:paper/sections/3_method.tex:93:In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2395:paper/sections/3_method.tex:96:Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2396:paper/sections/3_method.tex:100:with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2397:paper/sections/3_method.tex:103:Section~\ref{sec:experiments} shows the ensemble gain tracks a distributional quantity: the fraction of scores that land in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Direct is bimodal ($100$\% extreme), v6.5's looser JSON protocol produces $24$\% middle-zone outputs (a strong ensemble partner), v8's explicit refutation schema still lands $<1$\% in the middle. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} broadens the output distribution is the main open direction left by this work.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2401:refine-logs/EXPLORATION_JOURNAL.md:37:## Round 6.8 — Offline compose_ensemble (the actual elegant integration)
