review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2416:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection agent organised around three orthogonal axes---\emph{Tools} (descriptor, retriever, cropper, counter, knowledge lookup), \emph{Experts} (SubspaceAD, DINOv2 patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2 global), and \emph{Strategies} (direct, fusion, debate, interpret)---and an autonomous router that selects a (tools, expert, strategy) plan per domain via a single text-only VLM call. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} with two VLM backbones (SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B), the real end-to-end agent matches the descriptor-only direct VLM baseline on both backbones (Δ ≤ 0.5 pp, n.s.) and ties score fusion on SeedVL (Δ = $-1.1$ pp, n.s.) while trailing fusion by 5.4 pp on Qwen3.5; per-domain it posts double-digit wins on semantic / logical domains where the planner correctly avoids fusion (D6 LEVIR change $+13.4$ pp on SeedVL). A simulated calibration-only router (which executes the per-domain planned strategy without the noisy online expert override) reaches $+0.8$/$+1.6$/$+0.2$ pp over the strongest single strategy on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5 (SeedVL significant), and a zero-data descriptor router recovers $+1.7$/$+2.2$/$+4.9$ pp over direct for free. Along the way, we documented three orthogonal findings: task-anchored descriptors dominate any agentic intervention ($+6.4$/$+4.1$/$+3.2$ pp, all significant); simple score fusion is a very strong baseline that prior agentic work underreported; and six failed-variant ablations show that \emph{unconditional} second-call escalation hurts when the baseline is already strong.
