review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2687:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:11:A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2711:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:256:\caption{Budget-matched negative controls. \emph{v0 Direct, SubspaceAD fusion, and AnomalyClaw rows are on the test split} (same numbers as Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, repeated for comparison). \emph{``Random escalation'' and ``DINOv2 blend'' rows are calibration-split numbers} — these are disqualified designs we ran only on GPT-5.4 and SeedVL calibration, not on the test splits. Random escalation re-prompts a random 30\% of items; DINOv2 blend swaps SubspaceAD for a weaker patch-NN expert.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2720:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:303:Every test-split item has an analogous trace, serialised as JSON in the released \texttt{plan} and \texttt{raw\_output} fields. Score fusion, by contrast, produces only a scalar.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2744:I’m going to run one independent reviewer pass in parallel now, using the same repo evidence, while I do a tighter local diagnostic on the ablation confound. The key question is whether “middle mass” remains causal when the transformation preserves AUROC/rank exactly.
