review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:785:Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD), yet single-pass prompting fails silently on domains where the VLM's world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition. We study how to add structure on top of a VLM for \emph{cross-domain} VAD and identify two coupled failure modes in modern VAD agents: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---VLMs emit extreme scores (0.0x or 0.9x) with almost no middle-zone mass, which caps ranking accuracy; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---once the VLM commits to an anomaly hypothesis, tool calls tend to accumulate supporting evidence rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:787:We introduce \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free VAD agent that addresses both failures jointly. Its core component is a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that treats the query as \emph{normal} by default and lists up to three candidate features it finds unusual. Each candidate is then probed by a targeted tool (\texttt{side\_by\_side}, \texttt{reference\_profiler}, \texttt{reference\_retriever}) whose sole job is to \emph{find} the feature in the normal reference pool: a found feature is retired as natural variation, a surviving feature is an anomaly vote. This inverts the default deliberation direction and yields interpretable traces (candidate list, refutation verdicts, remaining features). We further show that \emph{ensembling} the agent's score with the VLM's Direct score exploits the complementary score-distribution shape between the two: Direct is bimodal ($100$\% $<0.2$ or $>0.8$), while the ReAct agent's JSON protocol naturally produces middle-zone outputs, so averaging creates rank-level diversity without extra VLM calls.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:789:On our \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} benchmark (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items, Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone), a simple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent}$ ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline ($0.7684$) by \textbf{$+4.52$ pp} (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta>0){=}1.000$ on $1{,}418$ items). The refutation agent (v8) ensembled with Direct reaches $0.7812$ ($+1.28$ pp) and provides interpretable traces; a triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ sits in between at $0.8036$ ($+3.52$ pp). On GPT-5.4 dev the same recipe generalises: the best triple blend over Direct, v6.6 self-ensemble, and v8 gives $+2.4$ pp (n=480). We localise the source of ensemble gain with score-distribution histograms: the $+4.5$ pp effect correlates with the agent's middle-zone mass ($24$\% for v6.5 vs $0.4$\% for v8), not with the agent's stand-alone accuracy.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:791:We release the Tool library, the v8 refutation agent, and the full $480$-item per-tool single-tool audit that motivates the design (thirteen tools audited individually on Qwen3.5 dev; all thirteen are net-negative when used in isolation but five contribute positive item-level flips when paired with Direct). Every headline number carries a paired-bootstrap confidence interval; the final v8 ensemble uses dev-only hyper-parameters applied once to test.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:818:Each tool has a small, auditable contract; all tools are zero-VLM-call. The \texttt{domain\_descriptor} returns the task-anchored anomaly specification for the current domain (resolving generic-prior/task conflicts, e.g.\ D08 ``planned urbanisation IS the anomaly''). The \texttt{reference\_retriever} ranks the reference pool by DINOv2-CLS cosine similarity and returns the top-$k$. The \texttt{hotspot\_cropper} reads top-$k$ patch coordinates from an expert and crops the anomaly hotspot (used by the \emph{interpret} strategy). The \texttt{component\_counter} runs connected-component analysis on the expert hotspot map (logical anomalies). The \texttt{knowledge\_lookup} retrieves a domain keyword list for the VLM prompt (e.g.\ \emph{lesion, haemorrhage} for medical). Full tool signatures, I/O contracts, and implementations are in Appendix~\ref{app:tools}.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:874:\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:875:\label{ssec:refutation}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:877:Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---the VLM emits extreme scores with almost no middle-zone mass; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:880:The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:882:    \item \textbf{Suspect list (turn 1, no tool).} The VLM outputs an \texttt{initial\_score}, a list of up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}~$=\{(\text{name}_i, \text{location\_hint}_i, \text{looks\_defect\_like}_i)\}$ and a \texttt{refutation\_target} index~$t^*$ into that list. The prompt fixes the default: if \texttt{candidate\_features} is empty and \texttt{initial\_score}~$<0.3$, the agent finalises at~$0.05$ without calling a tool.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:883:    \item \textbf{Targeted refutation (turn 2+).} The agent calls a refutation tool whose output is about \emph{whether the feature at $t^*$ appears in any reference}. \texttt{side\_by\_side} crops query and four refs at the same bbox; \texttt{reference\_profiler} returns a structured normal-baseline profile (object, colors, allowed variations); \texttt{reference\_retriever} fetches top-$k$ similar normals. Each output is paired with an explicit \texttt{interpretation} field containing a disconfirm clause, e.g.\ ``the bright region in the composite also appears in ref 2 at the same position.''
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:884:    \item \textbf{Verdict and update.} The VLM emits \texttt{refutation\_verdict} $\in \{\text{found\_in\_ref}, \text{not\_found}, \text{inconclusive}\}$, removes refuted features from \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}, and outputs \texttt{updated\_score}. If the remaining list is empty the score is forced into $[0.05, 0.20]$; if at least one feature survives the score is in $[0.40, 0.95]$ proportional to its defect-likeness. Remaining features are checked in subsequent turns until budget is exhausted (max 5 turns).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:888:In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:890:\paragraph{Ensemble with Direct.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:891:Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:893:  s_{\mathrm{final}} \;=\; w \cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + (1-w) \cdot s_{\mathrm{agent}}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:895:with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:897:\paragraph{Score-distribution diversity.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:898:Section~\ref{sec:experiments} shows the ensemble gain tracks a distributional quantity: the fraction of scores that land in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Direct is bimodal ($100$\% extreme), v6.5's looser JSON protocol produces $24$\% middle-zone outputs (a strong ensemble partner), v8's explicit refutation schema still lands $<1$\% in the middle. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} broadens the output distribution is the main open direction left by this work.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:910:The first pass already shows a narrative tension: the paper foregrounds v8 as the method, but the strongest result is v6.5 plus Direct, and v8 pure is substantially below Direct on Qwen test. I’m checking whether Section 4 and the artifacts hedge that tension clearly enough.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:927:(i) \textbf{DINOv2-PatchNN}: patch-$k$-NN expert alone. (ii) \textbf{SubspaceAD}: PCA subspace expert alone~\citep{subspacead2026}. (iii) \textbf{Direct VLM} (v0): one VLM call with task-anchored descriptor. (iv) \textbf{Debate}: proposer--advocate, 2 VLM calls. (v) \textbf{Interpret}: AnomalyClaw v1 three-route router, $\sim 1.3$ VLM calls. (vi) \textbf{Fusion}: $s = 0.8\,s_0 + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$. (vii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Descriptor Router}: per-domain rule $S(d)$ derived from the family taxonomy (\S\ref{ssec:router}; simulated by score look-up on the existing per-strategy outputs). (viii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Calibration Router}: per-domain argmax strategy chosen from 20 calibration items per domain (simulated by score look-up). (ix) \textbf{AnomalyClaw v3 (real autonomous agent)}: end-to-end agent that runs the autonomous-planner VLM call per domain and executes the chosen tool/expert/strategy plan per item with the online expert override. (x) \textbf{Oracle}: per-domain argmax computed on test labels (upper bound; cheats).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:937:\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
