review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:965:{\footnotesize $^{*}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){\geq}0.95$; $^{***}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$; $^\dagger$ GPT-5.4 v8 numbers are dev-only ($n{=}480$) because the sub2api endpoint repeatedly rate-limited v8's test-scale concurrency. See \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity} for the score-distribution analysis that explains the v6-vs-v8 ensemble gap.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:989:\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:990:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:992:\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:993:\label{ssec:v8_finding}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:994:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1000:\caption{Score-distribution diversity (Qwen3.5-VL-27B test, $n{=}1418$). The middle-zone mass $[0.2, 0.8]$ correlates with ensemble gain: a partner that emits more middle-zone scores creates more rank diversity when averaged with Direct, which is completely bimodal. v6 agent has $24$\% middle-zone mass and gives the strongest ensemble ($+4.53$ pp); v8 agent has $0.4$\% and gives a smaller ensemble gain ($+1.28$ pp).}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1007:Direct      & $0.449$ & $0.468$ & $55.3\%$ & $0.0\%$  & $44.7\%$ & --- \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1009:v8 agent    & $0.372$ & $0.421$ & $64.7\%$ & $0.4\%$  & $34.9\%$ & $+1.28$ pp \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1013:Direct emits no scores in the middle zone. An ensemble with a partner that does populate $[0.2, 0.8]$ creates ties within Direct's extreme clusters that the partner breaks in a signal-bearing way. v6's free-form JSON protocol naturally produces middle scores ($24$\%); v8's explicit refutation schema forces binary outcomes (all refuted $\to 0.05$ or surviving $\to 0.85$) so only $0.4$\% land in the middle. The ensemble gain on Qwen3.5 test correlates monotonically with middle-zone mass across our three ensembles. This suggests the next-generation refutation agent should emit \emph{graded} refutation-strength scores rather than a binary verdict---preserving the theoretical interpretability of v8 while capturing the ensemble benefit of v6.
