review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2577:   100	Direct emits no scores in the middle zone. An ensemble with a partner that does populate $[0.2, 0.8]$ creates ties within Direct's extreme clusters that the partner breaks in a signal-bearing way. v6's free-form JSON protocol naturally produces middle scores ($24$\%); v8's explicit refutation schema forces binary outcomes (all refuted $\to 0.05$ or surviving $\to 0.85$) so only $0.4$\% land in the middle. The ensemble gain on Qwen3.5 test correlates monotonically with middle-zone mass across our three ensembles. This suggests the next-generation refutation agent should emit \emph{graded} refutation-strength scores rather than a binary verdict---preserving the theoretical interpretability of v8 while capturing the ensemble benefit of v6.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2634:     1	Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD), yet single-pass prompting fails silently on domains where the VLM's world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition. We study how to add structure on top of a VLM for \emph{cross-domain} VAD and identify two coupled failure modes in modern VAD agents: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---VLMs emit extreme scores (0.0x or 0.9x) with almost no middle-zone mass, which caps ranking accuracy; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---once the VLM commits to an anomaly hypothesis, tool calls tend to accumulate supporting evidence rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2636:     3	We introduce \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free VAD agent that addresses both failures jointly. Its core component is a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that treats the query as \emph{normal} by default and lists up to three candidate features it finds unusual. Each candidate is then probed by a targeted tool (\texttt{side\_by\_side}, \texttt{reference\_profiler}, \texttt{reference\_retriever}) whose sole job is to \emph{find} the feature in the normal reference pool: a found feature is retired as natural variation, a surviving feature is an anomaly vote. This inverts the default deliberation direction and yields interpretable traces (candidate list, refutation verdicts, remaining features). We further show that \emph{ensembling} the agent's score with the VLM's Direct score exploits the complementary score-distribution shape between the two: Direct is bimodal ($100$\% $<0.2$ or $>0.8$), while the ReAct agent's JSON protocol naturally produces middle-zone outputs, so averaging creates rank-level diversity without extra VLM calls.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2638:     5	On our \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} benchmark (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items, Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone), a simple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent}$ ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline ($0.7684$) by \textbf{$+4.52$ pp} (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta>0){=}1.000$ on $1{,}418$ items). The refutation agent (v8) ensembled with Direct reaches $0.7812$ ($+1.28$ pp) and provides interpretable traces; a triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ sits in between at $0.8036$ ($+3.52$ pp). On GPT-5.4 dev the same recipe generalises: the best triple blend over Direct, v6.6 self-ensemble, and v8 gives $+2.4$ pp (n=480). We localise the source of ensemble gain with score-distribution histograms: the $+4.5$ pp effect correlates with the agent's middle-zone mass ($24$\% for v6.5 vs $0.4$\% for v8), not with the agent's stand-alone accuracy.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2640:     7	We release the Tool library, the v8 refutation agent, and the full $480$-item per-tool single-tool audit that motivates the design (thirteen tools audited individually on Qwen3.5 dev; all thirteen are net-negative when used in isolation but five contribute positive item-level flips when paired with Direct). Every headline number carries a paired-bootstrap confidence interval; the final v8 ensemble uses dev-only hyper-parameters applied once to test.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2652:     8	**Ensemble of Direct VLM + V6.5 ReAct agent gives +4.52pp on Qwen3.5
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2653:     9	test** (0.7684 → 0.8136, 12-domain macro AUROC, n=1418). The V8
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2654:    10	"refutation agent" gives +3.3pp on dev but only +1.3pp on test,
