review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2778:    14	(i) \textbf{DINOv2-PatchNN}: patch-$k$-NN expert alone. (ii) \textbf{SubspaceAD}: PCA subspace expert alone~\citep{subspacead2026}. (iii) \textbf{Direct VLM} (v0): one VLM call with task-anchored descriptor. (iv) \textbf{Debate}: proposer--advocate, 2 VLM calls. (v) \textbf{Interpret}: AnomalyClaw v1 three-route router, $\sim 1.3$ VLM calls. (vi) \textbf{Fusion}: $s = 0.8\,s_0 + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$. (vii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Descriptor Router}: per-domain rule $S(d)$ derived from the family taxonomy (\S\ref{ssec:router}; simulated by score look-up on the existing per-strategy outputs). (viii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Calibration Router}: per-domain argmax strategy chosen from 20 calibration items per domain (simulated by score look-up). (ix) \textbf{AnomalyClaw v3 (real autonomous agent)}: end-to-end agent that runs the autonomous-planner VLM call per domain and executes the chosen tool/expert/strategy plan per item with the online expert override. (x) \textbf{Oracle}: per-domain argmax computed on test labels (upper bound; cheats).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2788:    24	\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2798:    34	Direct VLM (1.0 call)                   & 0.846 & 0.800 & 0.768 \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2801:    37	Refutation agent (v8) alone (avg 1.1--2.2 calls) & $0.773^\dagger$ & --- & 0.671 \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2803:    39	\textbf{v6 agent + Direct ensemble} ($0.5/0.5$) &
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2805:    41	\textbf{v8 refutation + Direct ensemble} ($0.4/0.6$) &
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2807:    43	\textbf{Triple $0.5$ Direct $+0.1$ v6 $+0.4$ v8} &
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2809:    45	~~~~$\Delta$ vs Direct VLM (v6 ensemble) & $+1.74$ & $+0.93$ & $+4.53^{***}$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2810:    46	~~~~$\Delta$ vs Direct VLM (v8 ensemble) & $+0.42^\dagger$ & --- & $+1.28^{*}$ \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2816:    52	{\footnotesize $^{*}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){\geq}0.95$; $^{***}$ $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$; $^\dagger$ GPT-5.4 v8 numbers are dev-only ($n{=}480$) because the sub2api endpoint repeatedly rate-limited v8's test-scale concurrency. See \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity} for the score-distribution analysis that explains the v6-vs-v8 ensemble gap.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2840:    76	\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2841:    77	Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2843:    79	\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2844:    80	\label{ssec:v8_finding}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2845:    81	Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2851:    87	\caption{Score-distribution diversity (Qwen3.5-VL-27B test, $n{=}1418$). The middle-zone mass $[0.2, 0.8]$ correlates with ensemble gain: a partner that emits more middle-zone scores creates more rank diversity when averaged with Direct, which is completely bimodal. v6 agent has $24$\% middle-zone mass and gives the strongest ensemble ($+4.53$ pp); v8 agent has $0.4$\% and gives a smaller ensemble gain ($+1.28$ pp).}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2858:    94	Direct      & $0.449$ & $0.468$ & $55.3\%$ & $0.0\%$  & $44.7\%$ & --- \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2860:    96	v8 agent    & $0.372$ & $0.421$ & $64.7\%$ & $0.4\%$  & $34.9\%$ & $+1.28$ pp \\
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2864:   100	Direct emits no scores in the middle zone. An ensemble with a partner that does populate $[0.2, 0.8]$ creates ties within Direct's extreme clusters that the partner breaks in a signal-bearing way. v6's free-form JSON protocol naturally produces middle scores ($24$\%); v8's explicit refutation schema forces binary outcomes (all refuted $\to 0.05$ or surviving $\to 0.85$) so only $0.4$\% land in the middle. The ensemble gain on Qwen3.5 test correlates monotonically with middle-zone mass across our three ensembles. This suggests the next-generation refutation agent should emit \emph{graded} refutation-strength scores rather than a binary verdict---preserving the theoretical interpretability of v8 while capturing the ensemble benefit of v6.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2887:**Score**: 4.5 / 10 for NeurIPS/ICML
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2889:**Verdict**: not ready
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2891:This is a promising empirical project, but the current submission story is internally misaligned: the strongest result is the simple `Direct + v6.5` average, while the claimed method novelty is v8 refutation, which is weaker, partly unsupported cross-model, and has implementation/paper-trace issues. I would currently review this as a reject with useful empirical findings.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2893:**Ranked Weaknesses**
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2896:   v8 is positioned as the core contribution in the abstract, but on Qwen3.5 test it is much worse standalone than Direct: `0.6710` vs `0.7684`, and its ensemble gain is only `+1.28pp` with CI `[-0.08, +2.72]pp`. The winning system is not v8; it is `0.5 Direct + 0.5 v6.5`.  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2897:   **Minimum fix**: reframe v8 as an interpretable diagnostic variant, not the core method. Make the main contribution “score-diverse agent/VLM ensembling for training-free VAD,” with v8 as an ablation or interpretability case study.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2899:2. **The v8 implementation does not cleanly instantiate the described protocol.**  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2900:   The v8 runner still prompts old schema text like `h_normal`, `h_anomalous`, `initial_p_a` at [agent_v8.py:55](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_v8.py:55), summarizes assistant history using old keys and drops `candidate_features`, `refutation_target`, `remaining_candidate_features`, and `updated_score` at [agent_v8.py:127](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_v8.py:127), and later asks to “Update p_a” at [agent_v8.py:273](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_v8.py:273). This undermines the claim that every tool call has one legitimate use: retiring a candidate.  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2904:   The output file stores candidate lists and verdicts, but not full turn history, tool arguments, observations, or the evidence needed for a reviewer to audit whether a feature was actually refuted. The runner has `history`, but final JSON drops it at [agent_v8.py:323](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/benchmark/scripts/agent_v8.py:323). Also only about 46% of Qwen test items have any `refutation_verdicts`; most items are not producing a complete multi-step refutation trace.  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2905:   **Minimum fix**: save full compact traces for v8 and add a manual audit of 100 stratified examples: valid candidate, valid tool-target match, verdict supported by observation, final score consistent with remaining features.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2907:4. **Score-distribution diversity is plausible but not yet a defensible mechanism.**  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2908:   The Qwen evidence is only three systems: Direct `0%` middle mass, v6 `24%`, v8 `0.4%`. That is correlation with almost no degrees of freedom. It is also backbone-specific: GPT Direct already has middle-zone mass in the available files, so the abstract’s broad “Direct is bimodal” wording is not generally true.  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2909:   **Minimum fix**: add controlled transformations: binarize v6 scores while preserving sign, soften v8 scores with monotone/temperature mappings, add rank-correlation/error-complementarity metrics, and show which variable predicts ensemble gain better: middle mass, rank diversity, calibration, or standalone AUROC.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2912:   A 0.5 arithmetic mean is acceptable as a finding, but not by itself as a top-venue method. Worse, Qwen `v6+Direct` is essentially tied with the simpler SubspaceAD fusion baseline in Table 1: both around `0.814`, and the paper reports `Δ vs Fusion = +0.00` at [4_experiments.tex:47](/hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent/paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:47).  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2913:   **Minimum fix**: make the comparison against the strongest simple baseline central, not Direct-only. Report significance vs fusion, calibrated fusion, direct self-ensemble, prompt self-ensemble, score calibration, and a cheap two-prompt ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2915:6. **Cross-model evidence is incomplete for the v8 story.**  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2916:   GPT-5.4 v8 is dev-only in the paper, and the existing GPT test file appears unusable due to massive JSON failure. The abstract says the recipe generalizes on GPT dev, but dev-only evidence is not enough for a top-venue core claim.  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2917:   **Minimum fix**: either rerun GPT/another proprietary backbone test with fixed JSON reliability or remove v8 cross-model generalization claims. Keep only claims supported on test.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2920:   Sections about routers, oracle gaps, debate, interpret, fusion, v6, v8, and score diversity compete for the main contribution. Some router claims are hedged, but the main table mixes older agent/router systems with v6/v8, making the narrative feel accumulated rather than designed.  
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2923:**Is v8 Novel And Defensible?**
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2925:Conceptually, yes: “normal until survived refutation” is a clean and defensible agent protocol for reference-based anomaly detection. As implemented and evidenced, no: the current code does not fully enforce the protocol, v8 hurts standalone performance, the ensemble gain is weak, and the trace audit evidence is insufficient. It can be a nice secondary contribution after correction, not the lead contribution today.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2931:1. Fix v8 protocol/history saving.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2932:2. Rerun corrected v8 on Qwen3.5 dev and test, or at least dev plus a stratified test subset if full compute is tight.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2933:3. Add a controlled score-diversity ablation: v6-binarized, v6-softened, v8-softened, random middle-mass control, and rank/error-correlation analysis.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2934:4. Compare all ensembles against SubspaceAD fusion and Direct self-ensemble, not only Direct.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2938:A cleaner publishable version would say: “Training-free VAD benefits from score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not stronger standalone agents.” Then back it with test-set results across at least two reliable backbones, strong simple baselines, controlled score-shape ablations, and a corrected v8 trace audit. With that package, this could move into a 6.5-7 range.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2941:**Score**: 4.5 / 10 for NeurIPS/ICML
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2943:**Verdict**: not ready
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2945:This is a promising empirical project, but the current submission story is internally misaligned: the strongest result is the simple `Direct + v6.5` average, while the claimed method novelty is v8 refutation, which is weaker, partly unsupported cross-model, and has implementation/paper-trace issues. I would currently review this as a reject with useful empirical findings.
