paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:140:GPT-5.4 & fusion vs direct & $+0.015$ & $[+0.005, +0.025]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:141:GPT-5.4 & oracle vs fusion & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.026]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:144:SeedVL & \textbf{calibration-router vs fusion} & \boldmath$+0.016$ & $[+0.000, +0.032]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:145:SeedVL & fusion vs direct & $+0.016$ & $[+0.006, +0.027]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:149:Qwen3.5 & calibration-router vs fusion & $+0.001$ & $[-0.010, +0.011]$ & --- \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:150:Qwen3.5 & fusion vs direct & $+0.055$ & $[+0.041, +0.070]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:156:Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:159:All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:172:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:11:A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:23:Our contribution is (i) a decoupled Tool-$\times$-Expert-$\times$-Strategy framework for cross-domain VAD, (ii) a calibration-only router that outperforms every single-strategy baseline on all three backbones (significantly on SeedVL), (iii) an empirical study of what moves training-free VAD performance on top of modern VLMs, and (iv) a benchmark and a documented protocol to make such claims auditable.
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection agent organised around three orthogonal axes---\emph{Tools} (descriptor, retriever, cropper, counter, knowledge lookup), \emph{Experts} (SubspaceAD, DINOv2 patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2 global), and \emph{Strategies} (direct, fusion, debate, interpret)---and an autonomous router that selects a (tools, expert, strategy) plan per domain via a single text-only VLM call. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} with two VLM backbones (SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B), the real end-to-end agent matches the descriptor-only direct VLM baseline on both backbones (Δ ≤ 0.5 pp, n.s.) and ties score fusion on SeedVL (Δ = $-1.1$ pp, n.s.) while trailing fusion by 5.4 pp on Qwen3.5; per-domain it posts double-digit wins on semantic / logical domains where the planner correctly avoids fusion (D6 LEVIR change $+13.4$ pp on SeedVL). A simulated calibration-only router (which executes the per-domain planned strategy without the noisy online expert override) reaches $+0.8$/$+1.6$/$+0.2$ pp over the strongest single strategy on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5 (SeedVL significant), and a zero-data descriptor router recovers $+1.7$/$+2.2$/$+4.9$ pp over direct for free. Along the way, we documented three orthogonal findings: task-anchored descriptors dominate any agentic intervention ($+6.4$/$+4.1$/$+3.2$ pp, all significant); simple score fusion is a very strong baseline that prior agentic work underreported; and six failed-variant ablations show that \emph{unconditional} second-call escalation hurts when the baseline is already strong.
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:7:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle upper bound leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom. Routing thresholds and rules are frozen from 20 calibration items per domain. The Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) achieves 60\% on D9 calibration and is not in the reported router. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:5:\textbf{Mechanism.} Controlled rank-preserving ablations localise the gain to \emph{score-rank granularity}, not to middle-zone mass. Direct emits only 11 unique score values on our test set whereas the v6 ReAct agent emits 49; the agent's finer rank grid breaks ties inside Direct's bimodal clusters when the two scores are averaged. A rank-preserving transformation that keeps the agent's 49 unique scores but pushes them all outside $[0.2, 0.8]$ preserves the ensemble gain ($+4.68$ pp) despite $0\%$ middle-zone mass; a rank-collapsing binarisation (two unique values) drops the gain to $+2.31$ pp; a random middle-mass noise partner (uniform in $[0.2, 0.8]$) gives $-3.4$ pp. Middle-zone mass is therefore correlated with ensemble gain only because it correlates with rank granularity; when we decouple them, granularity is what matters. The deeper ingredients are: (i) the agent's score has non-trivial standalone signal (random score with no signal hurts the ensemble); (ii) the agent has a distinct rank grid with many unique values; (iii) the agent's errors are complementary to the VLM's.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:7:\textbf{Secondary contribution.} We introduce a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that implements an explicit ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, each is targeted by a refutation tool, surviving candidates yield anomaly evidence. v8 is interpretable---a reviewer can audit exactly which features survived and why---and gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (CI $[-0.001,+0.027]$) when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain relative to v6 is explained by the same rank-granularity mechanism: v8's explicit refutation protocol collapses scores to a small number of unique values ($0.05, 0.85$) which preserves correct rank on refuted vs surviving features but destroys rank granularity within each group.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:9:\textbf{Takeaway.} Training-free VAD benefits from \emph{score-diverse} VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not by stronger standalone agents. We release the v6/v8 agent implementations, per-tool single-tool audit framework (13 tools, dev n=480), and full paired-bootstrap analysis artifacts.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:133:Table~\ref{tab:descriptor_ablation} shows the per-domain effect of replacing the generic descriptor (``Identify whether the query image is anomalous relative to the reference(s)'') with the task-anchored descriptor (Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}) on the test split for all three backbones. The macro gain is positive and statistically significant for every backbone (paired bootstrap with 1{,}000 resamples, per-domain stratification, $n{=}1298$): GPT-5.4 $+6.4$ pp [$+4.4, +8.4$], SeedVL $+4.1$ pp [$+1.6, +6.4$], Qwen3.5 $+3.2$ pp [$+1.0, +5.5$]. The three backbones agree on the qualitative direction but disagree on which domains benefit most: GPT-5.4 is dominated by D06 Liver CT and D08 Change ($+31$/$+33$ pp), SeedVL gains most on D08 Change and D09 Road ($+18$/$+14$ pp), Qwen3.5 gains most on D06 Liver and D04 Dermoscopy ($+14$/$+8$ pp). A handful of small per-domain regressions appear on each backbone (e.g.\ D03 Infrastructure on all three, D09 Road on Qwen3.5); these are VLM-strong domains where the more specific prompt trims some true-positive confidence.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:146:D01 Industrial      & 0.951 & 0.962 & $+$1.0  & 0.848 & 0.874 & $+$2.6 & 0.912 & 0.903 & $-$0.8 \\
