     1	\section{Introduction}
     2	\label{sec:intro}
     3	
     4	Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD): a practitioner uploads a query image, optionally attaches one or two normal reference images, and asks the model whether the query is abnormal. This recipe drives much of the recent published progress on industrial~\citep{gu2024anomalygpt,jeong2023winclip}, medical~\citep{bao2024bmad}, and cross-domain~\citep{jiang2024mmad} benchmarks. The appeal is obvious: no training, no architecture changes, and a single VLM call per image.
     5	
     6	\begin{figure}[t]
     7	    \centering
     8	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_intuition.pdf}
     9	    \caption{\textbf{AnomalyClaw on CrossDomainVAD-11.}
    10	    \textbf{(a)} No single strategy wins uniformly across domains: the best direct/fusion/debate/interpret cell differs by family (industrial, medical, logical, GI, change, road). \textbf{(b)} A calibration-driven router matches or beats the strongest single strategy on all three backbones and captures 40--60\% of the oracle gap; a zero-data descriptor router recovers much of the gain for free. \textbf{(c)} Per-domain strategy assignment is well-aligned with the oracle (9--10/12 agreement across backbones).}
    11	    \label{fig:intuition}
    12	\end{figure}
    13	
    14	Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
    15	
    16	We argue that these three decisions should be explicit and decoupled. We introduce \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free VAD agent organised around three orthogonal axes:
    17	
    18	\begin{itemize}
    19	    \item \textbf{Tools} --- zero-VLM-call primitives (domain descriptor, reference retriever, hotspot cropper, component counter, knowledge lookup).
    20	    \item \textbf{Experts} --- frozen, pre-computed, non-parametric detectors (SubspaceAD~\citep{subspacead2026}, DINOv2 patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2 global).
    21	    \item \textbf{Strategies} --- inference schemes that combine tools, experts, and VLM calls (direct, fusion, debate, interpret).
    22	\end{itemize}
    23	
    24	A lightweight \emph{router} selects a (tools, expert, strategy) triple per image. The router comes in two flavours: \emph{descriptor rules} (one rule per domain family, frozen once from the benchmark descriptor, no labelled data) and \emph{calibration argmax} (per-domain argmax over 20 labelled calibration items). An online expert-signal override can promote any domain to the \emph{interpret} strategy when patch-$k$-NN detects a spatially concentrated anomaly that the VLM missed.
    25	
    26	\paragraph{Contributions.}
    27	\begin{enumerate}
    28	    \item We propose a decoupled \emph{Tool $\times$ Expert $\times$ Strategy} framework for cross-domain VAD. The framework is extensible (new tools/experts plug in via small contracts), cost-aware (routes aggregate to $\le 1.3$ VLM calls/image), and auditable (every decision comes with a trace).
    29	    \item We show that \emph{task-anchored domain descriptors} significantly improve all three backbones ($+6.4$/$+4.1$/$+3.2$ pp on GPT-5.4, SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B; paired bootstrap, $n{=}1418$, all 95\% CIs exclude zero) at zero inference cost; this finding alone changes the conclusions of recent agentic-VAD comparisons (\S\ref{ssec:descriptor_ablation}).
    30	    \item We give a controlled study of per-domain strategy variance: argmax strategy varies across domain families, and a calibration-argmax router beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone (point gains: descriptor router $+1.7$/$+2.2$/$+4.9$ pp over direct on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5; calibration router $+1.0$/$+3.3$/$+5.6$ pp over direct and $-0.5$/$+1.6$/$+0.1$ pp over fusion---with SeedVL fusion being significantly improved upon, 95\% CI $[+0.02,+3.18]$). An oracle per-domain selector caps achievable gain at $+1.3$/$+2.9$/$+1.5$ pp over fusion.
    31	    \item We release \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, a 12-domain-code benchmark (1{,}418 test items) with calibration/dev/test splits, the Tool/Expert/Strategy registry, the router implementation, and stratified paired bootstrap confidence intervals for all headline comparisons.
    32	    \item We document six failed agent variants on GPT-5.4 calibration---self-refine, naive debate, gated debate, evidence-injected proposer, third-call arbiter, normal-first---as concrete negative findings that motivate an \emph{adaptive} router rather than an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call.
    33	\end{enumerate}

