     1	\section{Method}
     2	\label{sec:method}
     3	
     4	We present \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD) agent for cross-domain deployment. Rather than committing to one inference recipe, AnomalyClaw organises the design space around three orthogonal axes:
     5	
     6	\begin{itemize}
     7	    \item \textbf{Tools} --- action primitives that shape the input or produce side evidence: domain descriptor, reference retriever, hotspot cropper, component counter, knowledge lookup.
     8	    \item \textbf{Experts} --- pretrained, non-parametric anomaly detectors that score the query against the normal reference set: SubspaceAD~\citep{subspacead2026}, DINOv2-giant patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2-giant global similarity.
     9	    \item \textbf{Strategies} --- ways of combining the VLM with experts and tools: direct VLM, VLM--expert score fusion, proposer--advocate debate, hotspot interpret.
    10	\end{itemize}
    11	
    12	A lightweight \emph{router} picks a (tools, expert, strategy) triple per image. The router has two modes---a domain-descriptor rule set derived from the benchmark family taxonomy, and a calibration-driven argmax selector that uses 20 labelled items per domain---both of which are frozen before any test query is seen. The agent runs the selected strategy, records the trace, and returns a scalar anomaly score.
    13	
    14	\begin{figure}[t]
    15	    \centering
    16	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_architecture.pdf}
    17	    \caption{\textbf{AnomalyClaw agent architecture.} A router reads the task descriptor and (optional) calibration statistics and selects a \emph{tool} prefix, an \emph{expert}, and a \emph{strategy}. Tools are zero-VLM-call primitives; experts are pre-computed and cached; strategies issue between 1 and 2 VLM calls. Average cost is $\le 1.3$ VLM calls per image.}
    18	    \label{fig:architecture}
    19	\end{figure}
    20	
    21	\subsection{Tool library}
    22	\label{ssec:tools}
    23	Each tool has a small, auditable contract; all tools are zero-VLM-call. The \texttt{domain\_descriptor} returns the task-anchored anomaly specification for the current domain (resolving generic-prior/task conflicts, e.g.\ D08 ``planned urbanisation IS the anomaly''). The \texttt{reference\_retriever} ranks the reference pool by DINOv2-CLS cosine similarity and returns the top-$k$. The \texttt{hotspot\_cropper} reads top-$k$ patch coordinates from an expert and crops the anomaly hotspot (used by the \emph{interpret} strategy). The \texttt{component\_counter} runs connected-component analysis on the expert hotspot map (logical anomalies). The \texttt{knowledge\_lookup} retrieves a domain keyword list for the VLM prompt (e.g.\ \emph{lesion, haemorrhage} for medical). Full tool signatures, I/O contracts, and implementations are in Appendix~\ref{app:tools}.
    24	
    25	\subsection{Expert pool}
    26	\label{ssec:experts}
    27	The expert pool is intentionally diverse so the agent can pick the right inductive bias per domain. Two experts are pre-computed per item and cached; both produce a scalar anomaly score and (for SubspaceAD) the top-$k$ anomalous patches.
    28	\begin{description}
    29	    \item[\textbf{SubspaceAD}~\citep{subspacead2026}.] Training-free PCA reconstruction residual ($99\%$ EV) on DINOv2-giant patch tokens at $672{\times}672$ (layer mean $\{-12,\dots,-18\}$). Strong on industrial texture, retail packaging, logical anomalies (MVTec-LOCO), liver CT, brain MRI; weak on remote-sensing change and infrastructure cracks where the expected ``normal'' span is too narrow.
    30	    \item[\textbf{AnomalyVFM}~\citep{anomalyvfm2025}.] Vision foundation model adapted via LoRA for zero-shot anomaly segmentation, with a convolutional decoder. We use the DINOv2 backbone variant; provides image-level score plus pixel mask. Complementary to SubspaceAD on infrastructure (calibration AUROC $0.72$ on D4 vs SubspaceAD's $0.50$) and on MVTec-LOCO logical anomalies where its synthetic-anomaly training data covers compositional defects.
    31	\end{description}
    32	A patch-$k$-NN side-channel on the same DINOv2-giant backbone provides $d_{\max}$, $\bar{d}_{\text{top5}}$, $d_{\text{base}}$, and a $48{\times}48$ hotspot map for the cropper tool; we did not use it as a primary expert in the reported results.
    33	
    34	\subsection{Strategy set}
    35	\label{ssec:strategies}
    36	\begin{description}
    37	    \item[\textbf{direct}.] One VLM call with the task-anchored descriptor and the first $k$ reference images. Cost: 1 VLM call.
    38	    \item[\textbf{fusion(expert)}.] One VLM call (direct), then post-hoc blend with the chosen expert's score: $s = (1-w)\,s_{\mathrm{v0}} + w\,\sigma\!\big((s_{\mathrm{exp}}-m)/m\big)$, with $w$ frozen on calibration ($w{=}0.2$ as the global default; per-domain $w$ in Appendix~\ref{app:per_domain_w}). Both \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)} and \emph{fusion(AnomalyVFM)} are first-class strategies.
    39	    \item[\textbf{expert-only}.] No VLM call; the expert score is sigmoid-rescaled around the calibration median and used directly. Selected for domains where the calibration AUROC of the expert exceeds the best VLM-based strategy by $\ge 3$ pp (e.g.\ D7 GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD).
    40	    \item[\textbf{zoom\_fusion}.] One VLM call, but the input is augmented with a high-resolution crop of the expert's hotspot region. Used when calibration shows the VLM benefits from a focused view.
    41	    \item[\textbf{debate}.] Two VLM calls (proposer + advocate); rule-based aggregation (Appendix~\ref{app:debate}). Used when calibration shows VLM confidence is poorly calibrated on a domain.
    42	\end{description}
    43	
    44	\subsection{The agent: per-domain plan}
    45	\label{ssec:router}
    46	For each domain $d$, the agent selects a (\emph{strategy}, \emph{expert}) pair $\pi(d)$ from the joint catalogue. We use a \textbf{calibration argmax with a fusion-default}: the default plan is \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)}, and we override only when the calibration AUROC of an alternative exceeds the default by at least $3$ pp (the ``override margin''). The 3-pp margin is itself frozen from a one-time sweep on a held-out backbone (GPT-5.4 calibration); we do \emph{not} retune it per backbone. Rationale: $20$ calibration items have a per-domain bootstrap noise floor of $\sim 2$ pp, so a margin slightly above the noise floor avoids over-fitting to small-sample noise.
    47	
    48	The plan is computed once per (backbone, domain) pair and frozen before any test query. At inference time the agent looks up $\pi(d)$ and executes the strategy; no per-image router call is made. The final cost is the strategy's cost for that domain (Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports an average of $0.92$ VLM calls/image on Qwen3.5).
    49	
    50	\paragraph{Why this works.} Three single-strategy regimes map to different per-domain optima: \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)} dominates texture / few-shot industrial, retail, brain MRI, liver CT, dermoscopy; \emph{direct} wins on semantic domains where the expert distorts the score (LEVIR change, SDNET infrastructure, road obstacle); \emph{expert-only} wins on domains where the expert is near-perfect (GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD AUROC $0.97$). A single fixed strategy cannot capture all three regimes.
    51	
    52	\subsection{Algorithm}
    53	\label{ssec:algorithm}
    54	
    55	\begin{algorithm}[t]
    56	\caption{\textsc{AnomalyClaw} v4 agent inference.}
    57	\label{alg:anomalyclaw}
    58	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
    59	\STATE \textbf{Input:} query $x$, references $\mathcal{R}$, domain $d$, descriptor $\phi_d$, frozen plan $\pi(d) = (S, E)$.
    60	\STATE $s_E \leftarrow \mathrm{Expert}_E(x, \mathcal{R})$ \hfill\COMMENT{cached lookup; 0 VLM calls}
    61	\IF{$S = \text{expert\_only}$}
    62	    \STATE \textbf{return} $\sigma((s_E - m_E)/m_E)$ \hfill\COMMENT{0 VLM calls}
    63	\ENDIF
    64	\STATE $s_0 \leftarrow \mathrm{VLM}_{\mathrm{v0}}(x, \mathcal{R}, \phi_d)$ \hfill\COMMENT{1 VLM call}
    65	\IF{$S = \text{direct}$}
    66	    \STATE \textbf{return} $s_0$
    67	\ELSIF{$S = \text{fusion}(E)$}
    68	    \STATE \textbf{return} $(1 - w_d)\,s_0 + w_d\,\sigma((s_E - m_E)/m_E)$
    69	\ELSIF{$S = \text{zoom\_fusion}(E)$}
    70	    \STATE $c \leftarrow \mathrm{HotspotCropper}(x, \mathrm{Expert}_E.\text{patches})$
    71	    \STATE $s_0 \leftarrow \mathrm{VLM}_{\mathrm{v0}}(x, \mathcal{R}, \phi_d, c)$ \hfill\COMMENT{1 VLM call with crop}
    72	    \STATE \textbf{return} $(1 - w_d)\,s_0 + w_d\,\sigma((s_E - m_E)/m_E)$
    73	\ELSIF{$S = \text{debate}$}
    74	    \STATE \textbf{return} $\mathrm{Debate}(x, \mathcal{R}, \phi_d, s_0)$ \hfill\COMMENT{2 VLM calls}
    75	\ENDIF
    76	\end{algorithmic}
    77	\end{algorithm}
    78	
    79	\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
    80	\label{ssec:refutation}
    81	
    82	Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---the VLM emits extreme scores with almost no middle-zone mass; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
    83	
    84	\paragraph{Protocol.}
    85	The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
    86	\begin{enumerate}
    87	    \item \textbf{Suspect list (turn 1, no tool).} The VLM outputs an \texttt{initial\_score}, a list of up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}~$=\{(\text{name}_i, \text{location\_hint}_i, \text{looks\_defect\_like}_i)\}$ and a \texttt{refutation\_target} index~$t^*$ into that list. The prompt fixes the default: if \texttt{candidate\_features} is empty and \texttt{initial\_score}~$<0.3$, the agent finalises at~$0.05$ without calling a tool.
    88	    \item \textbf{Targeted refutation (turn 2+).} The agent calls a refutation tool whose output is about \emph{whether the feature at $t^*$ appears in any reference}. \texttt{side\_by\_side} crops query and four refs at the same bbox; \texttt{reference\_profiler} returns a structured normal-baseline profile (object, colors, allowed variations); \texttt{reference\_retriever} fetches top-$k$ similar normals. Each output is paired with an explicit \texttt{interpretation} field containing a disconfirm clause, e.g.\ ``the bright region in the composite also appears in ref 2 at the same position.''
    89	    \item \textbf{Verdict and update.} The VLM emits \texttt{refutation\_verdict} $\in \{\text{found\_in\_ref}, \text{not\_found}, \text{inconclusive}\}$, removes refuted features from \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}, and outputs \texttt{updated\_score}. If the remaining list is empty the score is forced into $[0.05, 0.20]$; if at least one feature survives the score is in $[0.40, 0.95]$ proportional to its defect-likeness. Remaining features are checked in subsequent turns until budget is exhausted (max 5 turns).
    90	\end{enumerate}
    91	
    92	\paragraph{Why this counters confirmation bias.}
    93	In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
    94	
    95	\paragraph{Ensemble with Direct.}
    96	Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
    97	\[
    98	  s_{\mathrm{final}} \;=\; w \cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + (1-w) \cdot s_{\mathrm{agent}}
    99	\]
   100	with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
   101	
   102	\paragraph{Score-distribution diversity.}
   103	Section~\ref{sec:experiments} shows the ensemble gain tracks a distributional quantity: the fraction of scores that land in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Direct is bimodal ($100$\% extreme), v6.5's looser JSON protocol produces $24$\% middle-zone outputs (a strong ensemble partner), v8's explicit refutation schema still lands $<1$\% in the middle. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} broadens the output distribution is the main open direction left by this work.
   104	
   105	\subsection{Design principles}
   106	\label{ssec:principles}
   107	\begin{itemize}
   108	    \item \textbf{No training.} All VLMs and experts are frozen. SubspaceAD's PCA is a closed-form fit on reference features; patch-$k$-NN is a direct distance computation.
   109	    \item \textbf{No per-domain code.} The only per-domain artefact is the descriptor $\phi_d$ (one sentence) plus a family tag used by the descriptor router; both are written once.
   110	    \item \textbf{Bounded call budget.} At most 2 VLM calls per item; the average across CrossDomainVAD-11 is $\approx 1.3$.
   111	    \item \textbf{Auditable decisions.} Every output is accompanied by a trace listing the tools invoked, the expert selected, the chosen strategy, and (when applicable) the VLM's two-call evidence. Appendix~\ref{app:trace} shows a full trace example.
   112	\end{itemize}

