    76	\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
    77	Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
    78	
    79	\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
    80	\label{ssec:v8_finding}
    81	Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
    82	
    83	\subsection{Why ensembles win: rank-granularity, not middle-mass}
    84	\label{ssec:score_diversity}
    85	
    86	We test candidate mechanisms for the ensemble gain through controlled post-hoc transformations of the v6.5 agent's scores on the existing Qwen3.5-VL-27B test set (no new inference). Each transformation is designed to change exactly one property and freeze the others, so a change in ensemble gain isolates that property's causal contribution.
    87	
    88	\begin{itemize}
    89	    \item \textbf{v6-BIN}: collapse v6 scores to two values $\{0.05, 0.95\}$ at the per-split median. Preserves sign but destroys rank granularity (2 unique values vs 49 in original).
    90	    \item \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: monotone-remap v6 scores to linearly spaced points in $[0.05, 0.19]\cup[0.81, 0.95]$, preserving every unique rank but moving all 49 values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$. Preserves rank granularity AND standalone AUROC; destroys middle-zone mass.
    91	    \item \textbf{v6-AFFINE($a$)}: $s\mapsto\mathrm{clip}(0.5 + (s-0.5)\cdot a, 0, 1)$. Preserves rank for $a\!\le\!1$ (no clipping) and tests rank collapse near the boundaries for $a\!>\!1$.
    92	    \item \textbf{v8-SOFT}: monotone compression of v8 scores into $[0.25, 0.75]$. Adds middle-zone mass while preserving rank.
    93	    \item \textbf{RANDOM}: replace agent scores with uniform samples in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Pure middle-zone noise with no signal.
    94	\end{itemize}
    95	
    96	\begin{table}[h]
    97	\centering
    98	\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
    99	\label{tab:score_diversity}
   100	\small
   101	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
   102	\toprule
   103	Agent variant $X$ & $X$ alone & ensemble $\Delta$ & \# unique & mid-mass (\%) & $\rho_S$ & err-cos \\
   104	\midrule
   105	v6.5 original         & $0.7713$ & $+4.53$ & $49$ & $24.0\%$ & $0.535$ & $0.621$ \\
   106	\textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}  & $0.7713$ & $\mathbf{+4.68}$ & $49$ & $0.0\%$  & $0.535$ & --- \\
   107	v6-BIN ($0.05/0.95$)  & $0.6928$ & $+2.31$ & $2$  & $0.0\%$  & $0.449$ & $0.575$ \\
   108	v6-AFFINE($a{=}0.25$) & $0.7713$ & $+4.71$ & $49$ & $100\%$  & $0.535$ & --- \\
   109	v6-AFFINE($a{=}0.5$)  & $0.7713$ & $+4.64$ & $49$ & $100\%$  & $0.535$ & --- \\
   110	v6-AFFINE($a{=}2.0$)  & $0.7092$ & $+2.02$ & $49$ (many clipped) & $2.7\%$ & --- & --- \\
   111	v8 original           & $0.6710$ & $+1.22$ & $\sim\!15$ & $0.4\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.594$ \\
   112	v8-SOFT               & $0.6710$ & $+1.35$ & $\sim\!15$ & $100\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.634$ \\
   113	RANDOM                & $0.4845$ & $-3.39$ & $1418$ & $100\%$ & $-0.032$ & $0.522$ \\
   114	\midrule
   115	Direct only (reference) & $0.7684$ & --- & $11$ & $0.0\%$ & $1.0$ & $0.0$ \\
   116	\bottomrule
   117	\end{tabular}
   118	\end{table}
   119	
   120	Two clean falsifications and one corroboration follow. (i) Middle-mass is \emph{not causal}: v6-EXT-RANK preserves every v6 rank but pushes all values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$; the ensemble gain is $+4.68$ pp, larger than the $+4.53$ pp of the original. (ii) Rank granularity \emph{is} causal: v6-BIN collapses 49 ranks to 2 and the gain halves. (iii) v6-AFFINE is the cleanest positive control---compressing $a<1$ preserves rank and stays in the high-gain regime ($+4.64$ to $+4.71$ pp) regardless of middle-mass; expanding $a>1$ clips out-of-range values and collapses ranks, dropping gain. On this evidence, the mechanism we report is: \emph{Direct has a coarse bimodal rank grid (11 unique values); averaging with an agent that has a fine rank grid (49 unique values) breaks ties inside each bimodal cluster with real signal}. Middle-mass and rank granularity happen to correlate on unprocessed VLM outputs because free-form JSON produces many distinct intermediate numerals; decoupling them in the lab separates the correlational from the causal.
   121	
   122	\subsection{Per-domain behaviour.}
   123	\label{ssec:perdomain_main}
   124	The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).

