review-stage/AUTO_REVIEW.md:16:5. **Ensemble recipe too simple vs simple baselines.** → Table updated to compare against Fusion and Direct self-ensemble; reframe in abstract.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:47:~~~~$\Delta$ vs Fusion (v6 ensemble)     & $+0.87$ & $+0.14$ & $+0.00$ \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:77:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:172:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection agent organised around three orthogonal axes---\emph{Tools} (descriptor, retriever, cropper, counter, knowledge lookup), \emph{Experts} (SubspaceAD, DINOv2 patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2 global), and \emph{Strategies} (direct, fusion, debate, interpret)---and an autonomous router that selects a (tools, expert, strategy) plan per domain via a single text-only VLM call. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} with two VLM backbones (SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B), the real end-to-end agent matches the descriptor-only direct VLM baseline on both backbones (Δ ≤ 0.5 pp, n.s.) and ties score fusion on SeedVL (Δ = $-1.1$ pp, n.s.) while trailing fusion by 5.4 pp on Qwen3.5; per-domain it posts double-digit wins on semantic / logical domains where the planner correctly avoids fusion (D6 LEVIR change $+13.4$ pp on SeedVL). A simulated calibration-only router (which executes the per-domain planned strategy without the noisy online expert override) reaches $+0.8$/$+1.6$/$+0.2$ pp over the strongest single strategy on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5 (SeedVL significant), and a zero-data descriptor router recovers $+1.7$/$+2.2$/$+4.9$ pp over direct for free. Along the way, we documented three orthogonal findings: task-anchored descriptors dominate any agentic intervention ($+6.4$/$+4.1$/$+3.2$ pp, all significant); simple score fusion is a very strong baseline that prior agentic work underreported; and six failed-variant ablations show that \emph{unconditional} second-call escalation hurts when the baseline is already strong.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:38:    \item[\textbf{fusion(expert)}.] One VLM call (direct), then post-hoc blend with the chosen expert's score: $s = (1-w)\,s_{\mathrm{v0}} + w\,\sigma\!\big((s_{\mathrm{exp}}-m)/m\big)$, with $w$ frozen on calibration ($w{=}0.2$ as the global default; per-domain $w$ in Appendix~\ref{app:per_domain_w}). Both \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)} and \emph{fusion(AnomalyVFM)} are first-class strategies.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:256:\caption{Budget-matched negative controls. \emph{v0 Direct, SubspaceAD fusion, and AnomalyClaw rows are on the test split} (same numbers as Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, repeated for comparison). \emph{``Random escalation'' and ``DINOv2 blend'' rows are calibration-split numbers} — these are disqualified designs we ran only on GPT-5.4 and SeedVL calibration, not on the test splits. Random escalation re-prompts a random 30\% of items; DINOv2 blend swaps SubspaceAD for a weaker patch-NN expert.}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:266:\textbf{v0 + 0.2$\cdot$SubspaceAD fusion} & \textbf{1.00} & \textbf{0.835} & 0.813 & \textbf{0.851} \\
